[39;49m[K[2m• [22mComputer terminals began as electromechanical teletype machines used to send commands and receive printed output from[39m[49m[0m
[39;49m[K  mainframe computers. By the 1960s and 1970s, video display terminals replaced paper output with screens, making[39m[49m[0m
[39;49m[K  interaction faster and more flexible. Popular devices like the DEC VT100 helped standardize terminal behavior, escape[39m[49m[0m
[39;49m[K  sequences, and text-based interfaces. As personal computers became common, physical terminals declined, but terminal[39m[49m[0m
[39;49m[K  emulators preserved their command-line workflows in software. Today, terminals remain essential tools for programming,[39m[49m[0m
